I don't think there ever really was that convention in all of fantasy.
It was common, yes, but by no means universal.

Of course, "dwarven" (not "dwarvish"!), "dwarves" and "elven" are the
correct terms to use in discussions about NetHack anyway, but not
because of any external convention. They're correct because that's what
the game itself uses. The adjectives are specified literally in role.c,
and makeplural() in objnam.c simply does not have an exception for dwarf
to its knife/knives rule.
Funnily enough, the reverse case, input matching, _does_ allow for it:
if you set your fruit name to "pickled dwarf", you can wish for "3
pickled dwarfs", and you'll get "3 pickled dwarves". But not, even more
interestingly, for a "dwarfish cloak". "Dwarven" and "dwarvish" are
explicitly regarded as equal, but "dwarfish" is not.

role.c also gives the collective plurals for the races: "humanity",
"elvenkind", "dwarvenkind", "gnomehood" and "orcdom". I dunno, but I
don't like the last one, and having both elves and dwarves be called
"-kind" when the others all differ seems inelegant. Perhaps they should
be called "dwarvendom" and "orcery" instead.

